---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="For community studios"
	description="Kilnbook for community and shared-access ceramics studios: open-studio membership tiers, a shared firing queue members can check themselves, waitlists and equipment reservations."
>
	<section class="page-hero">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">For community studios</p>
				<h1>Open studio, without the clipboard.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Kilnbook runs the open-studio membership: wheel-time limits per tier, day passes, a shared
					firing queue members follow on their phones, and waitlists and reservations that sort
					themselves out.
				</p>
				<div class="hero-actions">
					<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
					<a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-ghost">See pricing</a>
				</div>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/studio-floor.svg"
				alt="A shared studio floor with six wheels, a kiln room, member shelves and a glaze table"
				width="560"
				height="360"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Memberships the way open studios sell them</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios don't sell classes so much as access: a monthly membership for wheel
			time, a shelf and a spot in the kiln. Kilnbook sets each tier up with its own rules. A Basic
			member might get 8 wheel hours a week, booked up to 3 days ahead. An Unlimited member books up
			to 7 days ahead with no weekly cap. Make evenings and weekends count double if the studio is
			always full after 5pm.
		</p>
		<p>
			Day passes sit alongside full memberships as their own tier, so someone trying the studio out
			can book a wheel for the day and pay firing fees per piece, without a shelf or a monthly
			commitment. Full members get the booking window, weekly hours and shelf space their tier
			includes. <a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>A firing queue built for busy shared kilns</h2>
		<p>
			When dozens of members are cycling work through the same kilns, the bisque shelf fills up fast.
			Members log each piece from their phone as they set it down, Kilnbook charges the firing fee by
			cubic inch (or from your presets: mug, bowl, large platter) and adds it to the right queue for
			bisque, cone 6 or cone 10.
		</p>
		<p>
			You see how full each load is, move pieces between loads, and mark a load as fired with one
			tap. If your top tier gets priority in the queue or a monthly firing allowance, Kilnbook keeps
			count so you don't have to. <a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>"Is my bowl out yet?" answered before anyone asks</h2>
		<p>
			Every member can see where their own work is: on the shelf, loaded, fired or ready for pickup.
			When a load is unloaded, everyone with work in it gets a text with a photo of the shelf. Pieces
			left for more than 30 days can trigger a polite reminder, then a storage fee if you want one.
			The front desk gets to talk about clay instead of checking the kiln room.
		</p>

		<h2>Waitlists and reservations for a rotating membership</h2>
		<p>
			With a large membership, the good wheel and the slab roller are always booked. Every shared
			tool gets its own calendar and rules: wheels, hand-building tables, the extruder, the spray
			booth. Set a cancellation window and what a no-show costs, and freed-up slots appear on the
			calendar right away.
		</p>
		<p>
			Workshops and classes you run on the side use waitlists that fill themselves. When someone
			cancels, the next person gets the seat offered by text and has two hours to accept before it
			moves on. A tablet by the door lets members tap in when they arrive, so you always know who
			is in the building while the kilns are running. <a href="/features/classes">Classes and waitlists</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Where to next</p>
			<h2>See the parts your members use most</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Kiln firings</h3>
					<p>
						Per-kiln queues, firing fees by cubic inch and pickup texts when the load comes out.
					</p>
					<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Wheel time</h3>
					<p>
						One-hour reservations for wheels, tables and equipment, with limits set per membership
						tier.
					</p>
					<a href="/features/wheel-time">Wheel and table booking</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Classes &amp; packs</h3>
					<p>
						Courses, drop-ins and class packs next to your memberships, with waitlists that fill
						cancelled seats.
					</p>
					<a href="/features/classes">Classes and packs</a>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Pricing</h3>
					<p>
						The Studio plan is $79 a month with unlimited members. Nonprofit studios get 30% off
						any plan.
					</p>
					<a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Give your members the queue, not the clipboard.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
